================================================================================
1.  SIBLING PARITY
================================================================================
1.1  THE SIX ARMS AGAINST EACH OTHER -- one real asymmetry, and it changes what
     the guard is FOR.

     Four arms write through a len-BOUNDED LOOP:
        TLB_A/B/C:  tlbi = ((ra & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff ; for(hwi=0; hwi<len; hwi+=2)
        TEX:        tlbi = ((ra & 0xff)  >> 3) & 0xff ; for(hwi=0; hwi<len; hwi+=2)
     A SHORTER len runs FEWER iterations, so a narrow access cannot over-run these.
     Max index: TLB 252+3=255 into [256]; TEX 27+3=30 into [112].  Safe.

     Two arms write FIXED ARITY, ignoring len entirely:
        LINEAR_A/B: tlbi = ((ra & 0x7f) >> 2) & 0x1f
                    d->re_linear_x[tlbi]   = idata >> 32
                    d->re_linear_x[tlbi+1] = idata
     len is not consulted.  A len=1/2/4 access at +0x7c gives tlbi=31 and writes
     index 32 into a [32] array.  8-byte alignment is what keeps tlbi EVEN and
     therefore <= 30 -- and MIPS supplies that alignment, not this code:
     cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c raises EXCEPTION_ADES/ADEL before the device is
     reached, so len==8 IMPLIES 8-byte aligned on the guest path.

     ==> SIBLING PARITY VERDICT: the six arms are NOT symmetric in what the width
         guard protects.  For TLB_A/B/C and TEX it is correctness only.  For
         LINEAR_A/B it is additionally the only thing preventing an out-of-bounds
         array write.  The fix must NOT paper over this: the LINEAR sites get an
         extra comment, the other four do not.

1.2  *** THE BRIEF'S PREMISE "load-bearing HOST MEMORY SAFETY" IS AN OVERCLAIM,
     AND I MEASURED IT RATHER THAN RATIFYING IT. ***

     Deriver: offsetof on the real struct, compiled and run (offs.c / offs.out,
     preserved in this dir):
        off re_linear_a = 1760 ; re_linear_b = 1888 ; de_reg = 2016
        re_linear_a[32] IS re_linear_b[0]  : YES   (+0 bytes)
        re_linear_b[32] IS de_reg[0]       : YES   (+0 bytes)

     The over-run lands INSIDE the same struct, on the adjacent register array.
     So the true characterisation is:
        - UB by the C standard (a write past the end of an array object), AND
        - a SILENT cross-array corruption: re_linear_a[32] clobbers
          re_linear_b[0]; re_linear_b[32] clobbers de_reg[0], i.e. a
          RENDERING-engine write reaching a DRAWING-engine register.
     It is NOT a wild write outside the allocation, NOT a host crash, and plain
     ASAN would not flag it (intra-object).  The comment I ship says exactly this.
     Shipping the brief's wording would have been an L4 wrong record in PRODUCT
     CODE, which is the worst place to put one.

1.3  AGAINST sgi_de's DIFFERENT ACCEPT SET -- deliberately excluded, said out loud.
     dev_sgi_re.c:692-694 is a SEVENTH live width kill:
        } else { fatal("sgi_de: len = %i not implemented\n", len); exit(1); }
     Its accept set is {8, 4}, not {8}.  Same class, different arm, NOT touched by
     this round; filed as `sgidewidth`.  A round named widthkillclass that repairs
     six of seven in one file and does not say so writes false unanimity.

1.4  *** AND A NEW FINDING THE PARITY CHECK TURNED UP: sgi_mte's WIDTH KILL IS
     DEAD CODE. ***  dev_sgi_re.c:1162-1178 reads

        if (len == 4) { ... } else if (len != 4) { ... } else { fatal(); exit(1); }

     The second arm's condition is the NEGATION of the first, so the final `else`
     is UNREACHABLE and the exit(1) at :1177 can never execute.  Two consequences:
       (a) the file has 8 width-guard SITES by text but only 7 LIVE ones;
       (b) sgi_mte therefore accepts len=1 and len=2 into its 8-byte path, writing
           mte_reg[regnr+1] AND mte_reg[regnr] from a one-byte access.
     Out of scope here.  FILED as `sgimtedeadwidth` -- reported, not fixed.

