================================================================================
3.  GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
================================================================================
PREDICATE reads : len ; d->warned  (the latch test !(d->warned & BIT))
BODY reads      : len (printed) ; d->warned (written |= BIT)
                  -- and NOTHING else.  The decline body touches no other state.

Where do they differ?  They do not: the guard's fields are a SUBSET of the body's.
There is no third state to build, because the decline body reads no field the
predicate does not.  Discharged N/A, WITH ITS REASON -- not unrun.

But `d->warned` is a SHARED BITFIELD, which is the question this check should be
asked instead (the #490 lesson: for a shared bitfield the real question is "who
else writes this field, and can they set my bit?"):
   DERIVER: grep -c 'd->warned' after the fix -> 12 (6 tests + 6 sets), ALL SIX
   pairs inside DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re), one per window, with no other writer in the
   file.  The field is NEW in this round, so there is no pre-existing writer to
   collide with.  Collision is therefore possible only by two sites SHARING a bit
   value -- which is M2, and `xsite 6msg` is its row.

