--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A1.  THE LEFTOVER, PROMOTED FROM A SENTENCE TO SIX MEASURED ROWS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The main loop and two review seats independently reached a disclosure this round
owes, and it follows directly from the 6-width/6-read pairing in section 0:

  *** THE WIDTH GUARD IS EVALUATED FIRST.  So after #497 a 4-byte read DECLINES
      safely -- but an ordinary 8-byte `ld' to each of the six fixed windows
      STILL ENDS THE HOST, because len == 8 passes the width guard and falls
      straight into the read arm's exit(1) (lines 245 262 279 296 346 362). ***

The pairing does not merge the two classes; it makes the LEFTOVER EXACT: fixing
six width kills leaves exactly six read kills at the same six offsets.  Without
this, a reader of #497 reasonably concludes reads were made safe -- the same
false-unanimity obligation as naming sgidewidth in 1.3.

I am NOT taking it as a sentence.  ROW LIST DIFF against section 5, stated
explicitly because #490 lost two prescribed rows to an undiffed rewrite:

   REMOVED : `ld readkill'      1 row , TLB_A only
   ADDED   : `<s> ld kill'      6 rows, one per window
   NET     : 30 -> 35 rows.  Every other row in section 5 is unchanged and
             none of its prescribed rows was dropped.

CHANNEL for the new rows (the section 4 obligation, discharged for them):
   kills      -- a silent removal of the READ kills, at any single window;
                 and any future merge of sgirekills into this round's scope
   observable -- process exit status AND the read arm's own text
                 "TODO: read from <SYM>", per window: exit1+txt vs exit0

A2.  WHY THE SPLIT FROM sgirekills IS CORRECT, recorded so it is not re-argued
   1. the fix designs genuinely fork: decline-return-0 vs implement-readback;
   2. DISPOSITIVE -- sgirekills has NO WITNESS, so the round-scope rule's joiner
      clause makes combining not merely undesirable but impossible today;
   3. merging on "the window still kills the host" proves too much: by that
      logic every unimplemented arm is one giant round, which is the #392 shape.

A3.  ADDRESS DERIVATION -- INDEPENDENTLY AGREED, NOT ASSUMED
   machine_sgi.c:500 passes base 0x15000000; dev_sgi_re_init registers sgi_re at
   baseaddr + 0x1000 for DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH (0x1000); the handler restores the
   full offset with relative_addr += 0x1000.  crmfbreg.h gives the six window
   constants 0x1000/0x1200/0x1400/0x1600/0x1700/0x1780.
   KSEG1: 0xb5001000 / 0xb5001200 / 0xb5001400 / 0xb5001600 / 0xb5001700 /
          0xb5001780 -- all inside 0x15001000-0x150017ff, cascade disjoint.
   The main loop derived these independently and they match the probe's SITES
   table BYTE FOR BYTE.  Agreement between two derivations, not a shared guess.
   The straggler row `other kill' uses 0xb5001800, the first address past
   LINEAR_B and still inside the device's 0x1000-byte registration.

A4.  ONE HONEST READING OF FLOOR CLAUSE (c), so nobody reports it wrongly
   The null medium (/bin/true) scores 12/35-equivalent, NOT 0.  Rows whose want
   is `exit0' pass trivially against a binary that exits 0.  THE DISCRIMINATOR
