ADDENDUM 2 -- written while the definitive table was RUNNING.  It changes NO
rows and NO instrument; it is analysis and two record corrections.  The frozen
probe md5 d04d6bc42baf0600e65c4417a535cb41 is untouched by it.
================================================================================

B1.  CORRECTION TO SECTION 1.4 -- THE ID IS `sgimtewidth`, NOT `sgimtedeadwidth`
     I proposed filing a new id.  It is ALREADY FILED as `sgimtewidth`, opened
     the same morning from a pass-1 reading, which records that the dead third
     arm is unreachable for every integer len.  I reached the same arm from the
     opposite direction -- a sibling-parity check on a different round -- so this
     is CORROBORATION, not a second defect.  A duplicate id would split one
     defect's evidence across two rows and let a future round close one while the
     other stayed open.  Section 1.4's finding stands; only its disposition
     changes.  What the re-discovery added to the existing row: the precise
     extent (:1162-1178, unreachable exit(1) at :1177) and the count -- EIGHT
     width-guard sites by text, SEVEN live.

B2.  DO THE ROWS NEED TO BE ASYMMETRIC, GIVEN THE ARMS ARE?   ANSWER: NO.
     Asked because section 1.1 found the six arms genuinely asymmetric -- for
     LINEAR_A/B the guard is the only bounds check, for TLB/TEX it is fidelity
     only.  The tempting inference is that the LINEAR sites need an extra row
     observing the cross-array write.  They do not, on two INDEPENDENT grounds,
     and the first is the one that generalises:

     (i) ROW DEMAND IS BOUNDED PER ENUMERATED MUTANT, NEVER PER PERCEIVED RISK.
         So the test is: is there an ENUMERATED mutant that reopens the LINEAR
         over-run and that no existing row kills?  Walking the closure:
            b_lina / b_linb    -> killed by `linear_x sw surv'
            d_ifnotelse        -> killed by `linear_x lw surv' (the declined READ
                                  falls through to the read kill).  This is the
                                  mutant that actually restores the over-run, and
                                  it is ALREADY caught -- by a symmetric row.
            d_andwrite         -> killed by `linear_x lw surv'
            d_growth           -> killed by `linear_x accept'
         Every enumerated mutant that reopens it is already killed.  And the edit
         that would widen the over-run directly -- `& 0x1f' -> `& 0x3f' -- is
         NOT in the clause-(d) closure at all: that closure covers predicates
         DOMINATING the edited lines plus guard-operand swaps, and `& 0x1f' is
         neither.  It is in the guarded BODY, untouched by this diff.
         A row added here would be owed by no mutant.  That is the addition
         licence's "cannot hurt" wearing detector clothes, and it is refused.

     (ii) AND IT IS NOT CONSTRUCTIBLE TODAY ANYWAY.  Observing the clobber means
         reading re_linear_b[0] or de_reg[0] back, and every read arm in this
         handler still exit(1)s.  This is section 2's claim-C5 EXPLICIT NO-ROW,
         restated: the aliasing is pinned by offsetof (section 1.2), not by a
         row, and the row is OWED TO sgirekills when that round makes reads
         observable.

     *** SO THE ASYMMETRY IS IN WHAT THE GUARD PROTECTS -- a fact about the
         SEVERITY of a regression -- NOT in what can reopen it.  Severity ranks
         the queue; it does not demand rows.  Conflating the two is how a row set
         grows without bound. ***

B3.  THE BRIEF'S WORDING, DISPOSED OF AS MEASURED
     Section 1.2's refutation is accepted by the main loop in full.  The shipped
     comment carries the MEASURED characterisation (UB + silent intra-struct
     cross-array corruption, invisible to plain ASAN) and appears at the TWO
     LINEAR SITES ONLY; the four non-LINEAR sites carry no memory-safety claim,
     because for them there is none to make.  Verified in the built source at
     lines 356-374 (LINEAR_A) and its LINEAR_B twin.

