ADDENDUM 3 -- two PROCESS findings from running the table, recorded while
table 1 was still going.  Neither changes a row.  Both are about the apparatus.
================================================================================

C1.  *** MY OWN FROZEN-COPY DISCIPLINE HAD A HOLE, AND I FOUND IT BY ALMOST
     FALLING IN. ***
     The frozen-copy rule says run a long job from a copy so the original is free
     to edit.  I froze the DRIVER (table497.sh) and the PROBE
     (sgi_re_width_probe.py) -- and pointed the driver's MUT variable at the LIVE
     scratchpad mutator, which it re-invokes ONCE PER MUTANT.

        MUT=/mnt/c/.../scratchpad/gx497/mutate497.py     <- not frozen

     So editing the mutator mid-table would have changed the instrument BETWEEN
     ROWS of one comparison, silently.  I was about to do exactly that -- to add
     the e_swapsite mutant described below -- and stopped only because I checked
     where MUT pointed before typing.

     This is the carrier's Python re-read hazard one level out: the rule protects
     the file you are thinking about, and the failure lands on the file you are
     not.  A driver freezes EVERY artifact it reads, not just itself.
     Fixed in table497_v2.sh, which runs /tmp/gx497_mutator_frozen.py, and which
     hard-exits if that file is missing rather than falling back to a live path.

C2.  A MUTANT ADDED BECAUSE THE CHANNEL CHECK SAID A ROW MIGHT BE VACUOUS
     -- and note this adds NO ROW, so section 5's row list is UNCHANGED at 35.
     Row demand is bounded per enumerated mutant; MUTANT demand is not row demand,
     and adding an adversarial mutant to test an existing row is free of the
     growth hazard the addition licence guards against.

     `xsite order' was prescribed as the ANCHOR row.  Re-reading section 4's
     CHANNEL obligation against the actual mutant list, every mutant that reds it
     -- e_wrongsite, d_sharedbit, d_bitcollide, every b_<site> -- ALSO reds
     `xsite 6msg', because each makes a symbol VANISH from the transcript.
     A row with no kill of its own is vacuous by construction, which is precisely
     what the CHANNEL line exists to catch at writing time.

     `e_swapsite' fixes that: it SWAPS TLB_B's and TLB_C's symbols, so all six
     literals remain present exactly once (verified in a dry run: declines=1 for
     each of the six) and only their POSITIONS are wrong.
        predicted:  xsite 6msg GREEN, xsite order RED  -> 34/35
     If instead both go red, my reading of the swap is wrong and I will say so.
     If `xsite order' stays GREEN, the row is vacuous and comes out or is
     re-cut -- an L1 pre-ship construction call, not a reopening.

