ADDENDUM 4 -- *** I ALMOST SHIPPED THE EXACT DEFECT I CAUGHT IN THE BRIEF, AND
THE CAUSE WAS MY OWN FIX. ***  Found while table 1 was running; corrected in the
source that table 2 measures.
================================================================================

WHAT HAPPENED.  Section 1.2 refuted the brief's "host memory safety" wording by
measuring the struct with offsetof, and the shipped LINEAR comments assert:

      re_linear_a[32] IS re_linear_b[0]   and   re_linear_b[32] IS de_reg[0]

Both were measured TRUE -- of the PRE-FIX struct.  My fix then inserted the new
latch field BETWEEN re_linear_b and de_reg:

      uint32_t re_linear_b[32];
      uint32_t warned;          <-- inserted here
      uint32_t de_reg[...];

Measured on the POST-FIX struct as first drafted (offs2.c / offs2.out):

      re_linear_b[32] IS de_reg[0] : NO   <-- THE COMMENT WAS FALSE
      re_linear_b[32] IS warned    : YES

TWO PROBLEMS, and the second is worse than the record defect:
  1. L4 WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE -- the same failure I had just refused to
     commit on the brief's behalf, reintroduced by my own hand, in the same
     comment block, within the same hour.
  2. WORSE ENGINEERING.  With `warned' in that slot, a LINEAR_B over-run sets
     WARNING LATCH BITS -- so the device's response to being corrupted is to go
     QUIET about being corrupted.  That is a strictly nastier failure mode than
     clobbering a DE register, and it was self-inflicted.

THE FIX: `warned' moves to the END of the struct, after mte_reg, with a comment
recording that the placement is load-bearing and why.  Re-measured (offs3.c):

      re_linear_a[32] IS re_linear_b[0] : YES
      re_linear_b[32] IS de_reg[0]      : YES
      re_linear_b[32] IS warned         : no
      ADJACENCY_AS_DOCUMENTED=YES

Verified additionally: syntax clean, and the ONLY non-comment difference from
table 1's source is the field's own line moving -- no guard-region code changed.

*** THE GENERAL LESSON, which is not about this struct. ***  An offsetof
measurement is a claim about a STRUCT AT A MOMENT, and a diff that adds a FIELD
invalidates it -- including the diff that cites it.  This is the carrier's
"an exemplar is a DATED MEASUREMENT wearing the clothes of an illustration",
reached from a new direction: here the measurement was invalidated not by drift
over weeks but BY THE VERY CHANGE THAT QUOTED IT, seconds later.

      A COMMENT ASSERTING FIELD ADJACENCY MUST BE RE-MEASURED AGAINST THE
      POST-DIFF STRUCT, NOT THE PRE-DIFF ONE, WHENEVER THE DIFF ADDS A MEMBER.

Cheap, mechanical, and it would have caught this in one command.  It is now a
step in table 2 (B3) that FAILS the run if the adjacency does not hold, so the
claim cannot silently go false again.

*** AND NOTE WHAT DID NOT CATCH IT: not the 35-row detector, not the floor
clauses, not the panel.  No row observes struct layout, and none should -- the
over-run is not guest-reachable while the guard stands, which is the whole point
of the round.  It was caught by asking what my own diff did to a number I had
measured 40 minutes earlier. ***

